// Generated by scripts/build-glossary-data.mjs from content-source/glossary. Do not edit by hand.
export const GLOSSARY_SHARD = Object.freeze({"satoshi":{"slug":"satoshi","cat":"basics","en":"Satoshi (sats)","contentLocale":"en","term":"Satoshi (sats)","aka":["sats","sat"],"short":"A satoshi (sat) is the smallest unit of Bitcoin, equal to 0.00000001 BTC, so one bitcoin contains 100,000,000 satoshis.","body":[["The unit is named after ",["Bitcoin","bitcoin"],"'s pseudonymous creator, Satoshi Nakamoto. Because BTC has eight decimal places, prices and balances can get awkward to read, so many wallets and apps let you display amounts in sats instead."],"Counting in sats makes small purchases feel more natural. Instead of saying you bought 0.00025 BTC, you can say you bought 25,000 sats. People who buy a fixed amount every week often talk about \"stacking sats\", meaning steadily accumulating small amounts.",["Sats also appear in fee settings. On the Bitcoin network, transaction fees are commonly quoted in satoshis per virtual byte (sat/vB). A higher fee rate makes it more likely that miners include your transaction quickly when the network is busy. Exchanges usually charge a fixed BTC ",["withdrawal fee","withdrawal-fee"]," instead and handle the on-chain fee for you."]],"example":["Suppose BTC trades at 60,000 ",["USDT","usdt"],". One satoshi is then worth 60,000 divided by 100,000,000, which is 0.0006 USDT. If you buy 30 USDT of BTC each week, you get 0.0005 BTC, or 50,000 sats, each time before fees. After 10 weeks at that same price you would hold 500,000 sats, which is 0.005 BTC."],"mistakes":["Miscounting decimal places and thinking 0.0001 BTC is 1,000 sats instead of 10,000 sats.","Assuming a satoshi has a fixed dollar value; it changes with the BTC price.","Setting a very low sat/vB fee in a self-custody wallet when the network is busy, leaving the transaction stuck for hours."],"faq":[{"q":"How many satoshis are in 1 bitcoin?","a":"There are exactly 100,000,000 satoshis in 1 BTC."},{"q":"Can I buy satoshis directly?","a":"You buy BTC, and any fraction you buy is simply a number of satoshis. Some wallets and apps also let you display your balance in sats instead of BTC."},{"q":"What does stacking sats mean?","a":"It is slang for regularly buying small amounts of bitcoin over time, often through a recurring purchase."}],"title":"What is a satoshi (sats)? Bitcoin's smallest unit","description":"Learn what a satoshi is, how to convert sats to BTC and dollars, and why fees are quoted in sat/vB.","related":[["bitcoin","Bitcoin (BTC)","Bitcoin (BTC) is the first cryptocurrency, a decentralized digital money with a hard cap of 21 million coins that runs on its own proof-of-work blockchain."],["dollar-cost-averaging","Dollar-cost averaging (DCA)","Dollar-cost averaging (DCA) is a strategy of buying a fixed amount of an asset at regular intervals, regardless of price, to spread your entry over time."],["market-order","Market order","A market order is an instruction to buy or sell immediately at the best price currently available in the order book, prioritising speed of execution over a specific price."],["cryptocurrency","Cryptocurrency","A cryptocurrency is a digital asset that is issued and transferred on a blockchain, secured by cryptography instead of a bank or central authority."]],"lesson":{"id":"E01","slug":"can-i-buy-part-of-a-bitcoin","ep":1,"title":"One bitcoin costs that much. Can I still buy some?"},"coin":{"slug":"bitcoin","name":"Bitcoin","symbol":"BTC"},"tool":null,"cta":false},"fomo":{"slug":"fomo","cat":"basics","en":"FOMO","contentLocale":"en","term":"FOMO","aka":["fear of missing out"],"short":"FOMO, or fear of missing out, is the anxious urge to buy an asset after its price has already risen sharply, driven by the worry that others are profiting without you.","body":["FOMO usually shows up when a coin is trending everywhere: big green candles, screenshots of gains and friends asking whether you bought yet. The emotional pull is strongest at exactly the point where the move is already large and the risk of a pullback is highest.",["In trading, FOMO leads to chasing: buying with market orders at the top of a spike, skipping your usual analysis, using more ",["leverage","leverage"]," than usual or putting in more money than you planned. Late buyers often provide the ",["liquidity","liquidity"]," that early buyers sell into."],["The fix is a process you decide before the excitement. Set a maximum ",["position size","position-sizing"]," per trade, decide entry levels in advance and place a stop-loss when you open. If you missed a move, it is fine to wait. There will always be another opportunity, but recovering from a large loss takes time."]],"example":["Suppose a coin rises from 1.00 to 1.60 ",["USDT","usdt"]," in two days and you buy 1,000 USDT worth at 1.60 because everyone is talking about it. The next day it drops back to 1.20, a 25 percent fall from your entry, and your position is worth 750 USDT. Had you set a rule to risk no more than 2 percent of a 5,000 USDT account, your stop-loss plan would have capped the loss at about 100 USDT."],"mistakes":["Buying with a market order into a vertical price spike.","Abandoning your planned position size because a coin is trending.","Treating screenshots of other people's gains as evidence that a trade is still early."],"faq":[{"q":"What does FOMO mean in crypto?","a":"It means fear of missing out, the urge to buy because prices are rising fast and you do not want to miss gains."},{"q":"How do I avoid FOMO trading?","a":"Write down your entry rules, position size and stop-loss before you trade, and only act when a setup meets those rules."},{"q":"Is FOMO the opposite of FUD?","a":"Roughly. FOMO pushes people to buy out of greed and anxiety, while FUD pushes people to sell out of fear and doubt."}],"title":"What does FOMO mean in crypto? Fear of missing out","description":"Learn what FOMO means in crypto trading, how it leads to chasing tops and simple rules to keep it under control.","related":[["fud","FUD","FUD, short for fear, uncertainty and doubt, describes negative news, rumours or opinions that make people worried about an asset, whether the concerns are valid or not."],["bull-market","Bull market","A bull market is a sustained period when prices rise broadly and investor confidence is high, commonly described as a gain of 20 percent or more from a recent low."],["position-sizing","Position sizing","Position sizing is deciding how much to put into a single trade so that, if your stop-loss is hit, you lose only a planned share of your account."],["fear-and-greed-index","Fear and Greed Index","The Fear and Greed Index is a sentiment gauge that combines market data such as volatility, momentum and social activity into a single score from 0 (extreme fear) to 100 (extreme greed)."],["stop-loss","Stop-loss order","A stop-loss order is an order that automatically closes your position when the price reaches a level you set, limiting how much you can lose on a trade."]],"lesson":{"id":"E16","slug":"position-sizing-basics","ep":16,"title":"Decide how much you could lose before you decide how much to buy"},"coin":null,"tool":null,"cta":false},"wash-trading":{"slug":"wash-trading","cat":"trading","en":"Wash trading","contentLocale":"en","term":"Wash trading","aka":["fake volume","wash trade"],"short":"Wash trading is when the same person or group buys and sells an asset to themselves to create fake trading volume and a false impression of demand.","body":[["In a wash trade there is no real change of ownership. One account sells to another account controlled by the same party, or a bot trades back and forth with itself. The goal is to make a token, an ",["NFT","nft"]," collection or an exchange look more active than it really is."],"Inflated volume can push a token up rankings, attract traders who look for busy markets, help meet listing requirements or make an exchange seem bigger. It can also be used to fake NFT sale prices. In many traditional markets wash trading is illegal, and regulators increasingly apply similar rules to crypto, though the details vary by country.",["For you as a trader, the risk is false confidence. A market with fake volume may have very little real depth, so your order can move the price sharply. Signs to check include volume that is huge relative to ",["order book depth","order-book"],", trades of identical size repeating at regular intervals, and volume that is much higher on one small venue than on major exchanges."]],"example":["Suppose an NFT is bought for 1 ",["ETH","ethereum"],", then sold between two linked wallets for 5 ETH, 12 ETH and 20 ETH within a day. The records now show 37 ETH of volume and a last sale of 20 ETH, yet the owner only ever paid 1 ETH and no outside buyer took part. A newcomer who pays 15 ETH because the last sale was 20 ETH may find no real buyers above a few ETH."],"mistakes":["Trusting volume rankings without checking order book depth or the spread.","Using the last NFT sale price as a fair value when sales went between linked wallets.","Assuming a token is popular because it shows large volume on a single small exchange."],"faq":[{"q":"How can you spot wash trading?","a":"Compare volume with order book depth, look for repeated identical trade sizes, and check whether the same wallets trade back and forth. Volume concentrated on one obscure venue is another warning sign."},{"q":"Is wash trading illegal in crypto?","a":"It depends on the country and the asset. Many jurisdictions treat it as market manipulation, and enforcement in crypto has been increasing."}],"title":"What is wash trading? Fake crypto volume explained","description":"Learn what wash trading is, why it inflates crypto and NFT volume, and the practical signs that a market's volume may be fake.","related":[["trading-volume","Trading volume","Trading volume is the total amount of an asset traded over a set period, usually shown as the 24-hour volume in coins or in USD value."],["liquidity","Liquidity","Liquidity is how easily you can buy or sell an asset quickly, in size, without moving its price much."],["proof-of-reserves","Proof of reserves","Proof of reserves is a report in which a crypto exchange shows that the assets it holds in wallets it controls are at least equal to the balances it owes its customers."],["market-maker","Market maker","A market maker is a trader or firm that continuously places both buy and sell orders on a market, providing liquidity and earning from the spread between them."]],"lesson":null,"coin":null,"tool":"/exchanges","cta":false},"whale":{"slug":"whale","cat":"trading","en":"Whale","contentLocale":"en","term":"Whale","aka":["crypto whale","whale wallet"],"short":"A whale is an individual or entity that holds or trades such a large amount of a cryptocurrency that their moves can noticeably affect its price or liquidity.","body":[["There is no official threshold. For ",["bitcoin","bitcoin"],", people often call addresses holding 1,000 BTC or more whales, but for a small token a holder of a few percent of supply can be a whale. Whales include early adopters, funds, companies, exchanges and token project treasuries."],"Whales matter because large orders can move thin markets, trigger stop-losses and liquidations, and change sentiment. Because blockchains are public, analysts track large transfers, especially big deposits to exchanges, which may signal an intention to sell, and big withdrawals, which may signal long-term holding.","Interpreting whale activity is tricky. Many large addresses belong to exchanges holding funds for millions of users, and a transfer between two wallets of the same owner looks like a big move but changes nothing. Treat whale alerts as one data point, not a trading signal on their own. Also check how concentrated a token's supply is before you buy it."],"example":["Suppose a token has 100 million coins in circulation and one address holds 8 million, or 8% of supply. The ",["order book","order-book"]," only has 400,000 ",["USDT","usdt"]," of bids within 5% of the 0.50 USDT price, which is about 800,000 coins. If that whale tried to sell even 2 million coins at market, the order would exhaust those bids and push the price far more than 5% lower."],"mistakes":["Treating every large on-chain transfer as a whale selling, when it is often an exchange moving funds internally.","Copying whale trades blindly without knowing their reasons or time horizon.","Ignoring how concentrated a token's supply is before buying."],"faq":[{"q":"How much crypto do you need to be a whale?","a":"There is no fixed number. It depends on the asset: what counts as a whale for a small token would be a modest holding in bitcoin."},{"q":"Can whales manipulate crypto prices?","a":"Large holders can move prices, especially in thin markets, and some manipulation does happen. In deep markets a single trader has less impact."}],"title":"What is a crypto whale? Meaning and why it matters","description":"Learn what a crypto whale is, how large holders can move prices and how to read whale transfers on-chain with care.","related":[["on-chain-analysis","On-chain analysis","On-chain analysis is the study of public blockchain data, such as transactions, wallet balances and exchange flows, to understand how a network is used and how holders behave."],["exchange-netflow","Exchange inflows and outflows","Exchange inflows and outflows measure how much of a coin moves onto and off centralized exchanges, and netflow is inflows minus outflows over a period."],["liquidity","Liquidity","Liquidity is how easily you can buy or sell an asset quickly, in size, without moving its price much."],["slippage","Slippage","Slippage is the difference between the price you expect when you place a trade and the average price at which the trade actually fills, usually caused by thin liquidity or fast-moving markets."]],"lesson":null,"coin":null,"tool":"/onchain","cta":false},"margin-call":{"slug":"margin-call","cat":"derivatives","en":"Margin call","contentLocale":"en","term":"Margin call","aka":["margin warning","margin alert"],"short":"A margin call is a warning that your collateral has fallen close to the minimum required for a leveraged position, so you need to add margin or reduce the position to avoid liquidation.","body":[["In traditional finance, a broker calls you to demand more collateral. On crypto exchanges, a margin call is usually an automatic app notification, email or on-screen alert when your margin ratio passes a warning level. It is not a request you must answer; if you do nothing and the price keeps moving, ",["liquidation","liquidation"]," follows automatically."],["You have three main responses. You can add collateral, which moves your ",["liquidation price","liquidation-price"]," further away. You can close part of the position, which lowers the margin required. Or you can close the whole position and accept the loss. Doing nothing is also a choice, and it lets the liquidation engine decide for you."],"Warning thresholds, notification channels and timing differ by exchange, and fast markets can move from warning to liquidation within seconds. Treat a margin call as evidence that your position was too large or your stop-loss too far away, rather than a reason to keep adding money to a losing trade."],"example":["Suppose you hold an isolated 10x long worth 15,000 ",["USDT","usdt"]," with 1,500 USDT of margin, and the exchange warns you when your margin balance falls below half of the ",["initial margin","initial-margin"],". After a 6% drop, your unrealized loss is 900 USDT and your margin balance is 600 USDT. You receive a margin call. Adding 600 USDT pushes your liquidation price roughly 4 percentage points lower; closing half the position cuts the required margin in half."],"mistakes":["Repeatedly adding margin to a losing position instead of rethinking the trade.","Relying on notifications arriving in time during a fast move.","Assuming a margin call gives you a fixed grace period before liquidation."],"faq":[{"q":"Do crypto exchanges give margin calls?","a":"Most send automatic warnings when your margin ratio gets high, but the exchange will liquidate without waiting for you to act."},{"q":"Should I add margin after a margin call?","a":"Only if your original trade idea is still valid and the added amount fits your risk plan. Often reducing the position is the safer choice."}],"title":"What is a margin call in crypto? What to do next","description":"Learn what a margin call means on a crypto exchange, how it leads to liquidation and the options you have when you get one.","related":[["maintenance-margin","Maintenance margin","Maintenance margin is the minimum collateral you must keep in a leveraged position; if your margin falls below it, the position is liquidated."],["liquidation","Liquidation","Liquidation is when an exchange forcibly closes your leveraged position because your margin has fallen below the maintenance margin requirement."],["cross-margin","Cross margin","Cross margin is a margin mode where all your open positions share the available balance in your account as collateral, so profits and spare funds can absorb losses before liquidation."],["leverage","Leverage","Leverage is using borrowed funds or margin to open a position larger than your own capital, which multiplies both profits and losses."]],"lesson":{"id":"E20","slug":"maintenance-margin","ep":20,"title":"Why you can be closed out before your margin hits zero"},"coin":null,"tool":"/liquidations","cta":false},"liquidation":{"slug":"liquidation","cat":"derivatives","en":"Liquidation","contentLocale":"en","term":"Liquidation","aka":["forced liquidation","getting liquidated","rekt"],"short":"Liquidation is when an exchange forcibly closes your leveraged position because your margin has fallen below the maintenance margin requirement.","body":[["Every leveraged position has a point where your remaining margin is no longer enough to cover further losses. When the ",["mark price","mark-price"]," reaches that point, the exchange's liquidation engine takes over the position and closes it, either all at once or in steps for larger positions. You usually lose most or all of the margin for that position, and many exchanges also charge a liquidation fee."],["Liquidation exists to protect the exchange and other traders from losses larger than the collateral. If the engine closes a position at a better price than the bankruptcy price, the surplus often goes to the ",["insurance fund","insurance-fund"],". If the market moves too fast, the insurance fund covers the shortfall, and in extreme cases ",["auto-deleveraging (ADL)","auto-deleveraging"]," reduces profitable opposing positions."],["Large waves of liquidations can feed on themselves: forced selling pushes price lower, which triggers more liquidations. This is why crypto sometimes moves very fast. To avoid liquidation, use lower ",["leverage","leverage"],", keep a stop-loss well before the ",["liquidation price","liquidation-price"],", and size positions so one move cannot wipe you out."]],"example":["Suppose you open a 20x long on ",["BTC","bitcoin"]," at 50,000 USDT with 1,000 USDT of margin, a 20,000 USDT position. A 1% drop costs you 200 USDT. With a maintenance margin rate of 0.5%, the position is liquidated after a drop of roughly 4.5%, near 47,750, when your margin has shrunk to about 100 USDT. If a stop-loss at 49,000 had triggered first, you would have lost about 400 USDT instead of almost all 1,000."],"mistakes":["Treating the liquidation price as your stop-loss.","Watching the last price on a chart while the exchange liquidates on the mark price.","Re-entering with higher leverage straight after being liquidated to win back the loss."],"faq":[{"q":"What does it mean to get liquidated in crypto?","a":"Your leveraged position was closed by the exchange because losses used up your margin. The collateral for that position is mostly or fully lost."},{"q":"Can I get my money back after liquidation?","a":"No. Liquidation is final and the lost margin is not returned, which is why a stop-loss is important."}],"title":"What is liquidation in crypto? Why positions get closed","description":"Learn what crypto liquidation is, what triggers it, what happens to your margin and how to keep it from happening.","related":[["liquidation-price","Liquidation price","The liquidation price is the mark price at which your leveraged position will be forcibly closed because your margin would fall to the maintenance margin level."],["maintenance-margin","Maintenance margin","Maintenance margin is the minimum collateral you must keep in a leveraged position; if your margin falls below it, the position is liquidated."],["mark-price","Mark price","The mark price is a fair-value price an exchange uses to calculate unrealized profit and loss and to trigger liquidations, instead of the last traded price."],["insurance-fund","Insurance fund","An insurance fund is a reserve an exchange keeps to cover losses when liquidated positions cannot be closed at a price good enough to repay what they owe."],["auto-deleveraging","Auto-deleveraging (ADL)","Auto-deleveraging (ADL) is a last-resort mechanism where an exchange automatically reduces profitable opposing positions to cover a liquidation that the market and insurance fund cannot absorb."]],"lesson":{"id":"E23","slug":"liquidation-mark-price","ep":23,"title":"Liquidation: who pressed the forced-close button?"},"coin":null,"tool":"/liquidations","cta":false},"liquidation-price":{"slug":"liquidation-price","cat":"derivatives","en":"Liquidation price","contentLocale":"en","term":"Liquidation price","aka":["liq price","liquidation level"],"short":"The liquidation price is the mark price at which your leveraged position will be forcibly closed because your margin would fall to the maintenance margin level.","body":[["Exchanges show an estimated liquidation price for every open position. For a long, it sits below your entry; for a short, above it. The distance depends mainly on ",["leverage","leverage"],": higher leverage means less room. ",["Maintenance margin rate","maintenance-margin"],", fees and, in ",["cross mode","cross-margin"],", your other balance and positions also affect it."],"A simplified formula for an isolated long is entry × (1 − 1 ÷ leverage + maintenance margin rate). For a short it is entry × (1 + 1 ÷ leverage − maintenance margin rate). Real numbers differ slightly because of fees, funding and tiered maintenance rates, so rely on the figure your exchange shows.",["The liquidation price is not fixed. Adding margin moves it further away. Funding payments and partial closes can move it, and in cross mode any change to your balance or other positions changes it. The trigger is the ",["mark price","mark-price"],", which is designed to resist brief spikes on a single exchange."]],"example":["Suppose you open an isolated 10x long on ",["BTC","bitcoin"]," at 60,000 ",["USDT","usdt"]," with a maintenance margin rate of 0.5%. Using the simplified formula, the liquidation price is 60,000 × (1 − 0.1 + 0.005) = 54,300 USDT, about 9.5% below entry. At 25x the same formula gives 60,000 × (1 − 0.04 + 0.005) = 57,900, only 3.5% away. A 10x short from 60,000 would be liquidated near 65,700."],"mistakes":["Assuming the liquidation price never changes after opening the trade.","Comparing it with the last traded price instead of the mark price.","Placing a stop-loss so close to the liquidation price that slippage could carry the fill past it."],"faq":[{"q":"How do I move my liquidation price further away?","a":"Add margin, reduce the position size, or use lower leverage when opening. In cross mode, a larger free balance also helps."},{"q":"Why was I liquidated before the chart reached my liquidation price?","a":"Usually because the mark price, not the last price on the chart, touched the level, or because fees and funding shifted the liquidation price."}],"title":"What is liquidation price? How it is calculated","description":"Learn what a liquidation price is, how leverage and maintenance margin set it, and how to calculate it for longs and shorts.","related":[["liquidation","Liquidation","Liquidation is when an exchange forcibly closes your leveraged position because your margin has fallen below the maintenance margin requirement."],["leverage","Leverage","Leverage is using borrowed funds or margin to open a position larger than your own capital, which multiplies both profits and losses."],["isolated-margin","Isolated margin","Isolated margin is a margin mode where each position has its own separate collateral, so a liquidation can only take the margin assigned to that position."],["mark-price","Mark price","The mark price is a fair-value price an exchange uses to calculate unrealized profit and loss and to trigger liquidations, instead of the last traded price."],["maintenance-margin","Maintenance margin","Maintenance margin is the minimum collateral you must keep in a leveraged position; if your margin falls below it, the position is liquidated."]],"lesson":{"id":"E23","slug":"liquidation-mark-price","ep":23,"title":"Liquidation: who pressed the forced-close button?"},"coin":null,"tool":"/liquidations","cta":false},"long-short-ratio":{"slug":"long-short-ratio","cat":"derivatives","en":"Long/short ratio","contentLocale":"en","term":"Long/short ratio","aka":["long short ratio","L/S ratio"],"short":"The long/short ratio compares how many traders or how much position size on an exchange is long versus short on a futures market, as a gauge of sentiment.","body":[["Because every ",["futures contract","futures-contract"]," has a buyer and a seller, total long and short contracts are always equal. So the long/short ratio usually measures something narrower: the share of accounts holding net long positions versus net short ones, or the positions of the exchange's top traders. A ratio of 2 means twice as many accounts are long as short."],"Exchanges publish different versions, such as all accounts, top-trader accounts and top-trader positions. These can tell different stories, for example many small accounts long while large accounts lean short. Read the definition on each exchange before comparing numbers.",["Traders often use extreme readings as a contrarian hint: when almost everyone is long, there may be few buyers left and many potential forced sellers. That is not a timing signal. Combine it with funding, ",["open interest","open-interest"]," and price structure rather than trading it alone."]],"example":["Suppose an exchange reports that 70% of accounts on an ",["ETH","ethereum"]," perpetual are net long, a long/short ratio of 70 ÷ 30 ≈ 2.33. Because total longs equal total shorts in size, the 30% of short accounts must hold larger average positions. If price drops 4% and long liquidations pile up, that crowded long side can push the fall further."],"mistakes":["Assuming a high ratio means more money is long than short; in contracts, they are always equal.","Comparing an account-based ratio on one exchange with a position-based ratio on another.","Trading the ratio as a direct buy or sell signal."],"faq":[{"q":"Is a high long/short ratio bullish?","a":"It shows that many accounts lean long, which can be a crowding warning rather than a bullish sign. It should be read together with funding and open interest."},{"q":"Why do longs and shorts not balance in the ratio?","a":"Because most ratios count accounts, not contract size. Total long and short contracts are always equal."}],"title":"What is the long/short ratio in crypto? Explained","description":"Learn what the long/short ratio measures, why longs and shorts always match in size, and how traders read crowded positioning.","related":[["open-interest","Open interest","Open interest is the total number or value of derivatives contracts, such as futures or options, that are currently open and not yet closed or settled."],["funding-rate","Funding rate","A funding rate is a periodic payment between long and short traders that keeps a perpetual futures price close to the spot price."],["long-and-short","Long and short positions","A long position profits when an asset's price rises, and a short position profits when its price falls."],["short-squeeze","Short squeeze","A short squeeze is a rapid price rise that forces short sellers to buy back their positions, through stop-losses or liquidation, which pushes the price even higher."]],"lesson":null,"coin":null,"tool":"/funding-rates","cta":false},"anti-phishing-code":{"slug":"anti-phishing-code","cat":"wallets-security","en":"Anti-phishing code","contentLocale":"en","term":"Anti-phishing code","aka":["anti-phishing phrase"],"short":"An anti-phishing code is a personal word or phrase you set in your exchange account that the exchange includes in its genuine emails, so you can spot fake messages that do not contain it.","body":[[["Phishing","phishing"]," emails often copy an exchange's logo, layout and sender name perfectly. What a scammer does not know is the private code you chose in your security settings. Exchanges such as Binance, OKX, Bybit and Bitget offer this feature, and some show the code in other official messages too."],"When an email claims to be from your exchange, look for your code first. If it is missing or wrong, treat the email as fake, do not click any links, and open the exchange app directly instead.","The code only proves an email is real; it does not make links inside a real email safe to follow blindly, and scammers can still reach you through SMS, social media or calls, which usually do not carry the code. Choose something that is not easy to guess and do not share it."],"example":["Say you set the anti-phishing code BlueKettle42 on your exchange. One morning you get an email saying a withdrawal of 0.4 ",["ETH","ethereum"]," is pending and you must cancel it within 15 minutes. The design looks right, but there is no BlueKettle42 anywhere. You ignore the link, open the exchange app and see no pending withdrawal. The email was a phishing attempt."],"mistakes":["Setting the code but never actually checking for it when an urgent email arrives.","Assuming a message on Telegram or SMS is real because your email has a code.","Choosing an obvious code such as your name or the exchange name."],"faq":[{"q":"Where do I set an anti-phishing code?","a":"Usually in the security section of your exchange account settings. The exact menu name differs between exchanges."},{"q":"Does an anti-phishing code stop all scams?","a":"No. It helps you verify emails, but you still need 2FA, a withdrawal whitelist and careful checking of links and addresses."}],"title":"What is an anti-phishing code? How to use it","description":"Learn what an exchange anti-phishing code is, how it helps you spot fake emails, and what it does not protect against.","related":[["phishing","Phishing","Phishing is a scam where attackers impersonate a trusted exchange, wallet or person to trick you into revealing login details or a seed phrase, or into signing a transaction that hands over your funds."],["two-factor-authentication","Two-factor authentication (2FA)","Two-factor authentication (2FA) is a login and approval method that asks for a second proof, such as a code from an authenticator app or a security key, in addition to your password."],["withdrawal-whitelist","Withdrawal whitelist","A withdrawal whitelist is an exchange security setting that only allows withdrawals to addresses you have saved and approved in advance, blocking transfers to any new address."]],"lesson":{"id":"E04","slug":"find-the-official-exchange","ep":4,"title":"Find the real door first: don't open an account on a fake platform"},"coin":null,"tool":null,"cta":false},"pig-butchering-scam":{"slug":"pig-butchering-scam","cat":"wallets-security","en":"Pig-butchering scam","contentLocale":"en","term":"Pig-butchering scam","aka":["pig butchering","romance investment scam","sha zhu pan"],"short":"A pig-butchering scam is a long-con fraud where a stranger builds trust over weeks, often as a friend or romantic interest, then steers you into a fake crypto investment platform and takes everything you deposit.","body":["The name comes from the Chinese phrase sha zhu pan, describing fattening a pig before slaughter. It often starts with a wrong-number text, a dating app match or a social media message. The scammer is friendly and patient, shares lifestyle photos and mentions trading profits only casually at first.","Next you are shown an app or website that looks like a real trading platform. Small first deposits appear to grow fast, and an early small withdrawal may even work to build trust. When you deposit larger sums, the balance keeps rising on screen, but any large withdrawal is blocked by sudden taxes, fees or verification charges.","Warning signs: someone you have never met pushing a specific platform, guaranteed or unusually steady returns, a platform not available on official app stores, and pressure to pay fees before you can withdraw."],"example":["Suppose a new online friend helps you open an account on a trading site and you deposit 1,000 ",["USDT","usdt"],". Within two weeks the dashboard shows 1,400 USDT, and a 100 USDT withdrawal works. You add 10,000 USDT. The balance shows 15,000 USDT, but withdrawing it requires a 20 percent tax payment of 3,000 USDT first. Paying it only leads to another fee. The 11,000 USDT you sent was never traded at all."],"mistakes":["Trusting a trading platform because a friend you have only met online recommends it.","Treating a small successful withdrawal as proof that the platform is legitimate.","Paying an unlock fee or tax to release a balance, which only increases the loss."],"faq":[{"q":"How do I check if a trading platform is real?","a":"Look it up independently, check whether the regulator in your country lists it, and be wary of apps installed from links rather than official stores."},{"q":"Can I get my money back from a pig-butchering scam?","a":"Recovery is difficult. Report it to the police and the exchange you sent from quickly, keep all records, and ignore recovery services that ask for upfront fees, which are often a second scam."}],"title":"What is a pig-butchering scam? Warning signs","description":"How pig-butchering crypto scams build trust, how fake platforms block withdrawals, and the warning signs to check.","related":[["phishing","Phishing","Phishing is a scam where attackers impersonate a trusted exchange, wallet or person to trick you into revealing login details or a seed phrase, or into signing a transaction that hands over your funds."],["rug-pull","Rug pull","A rug pull is a scam where the creators of a crypto project suddenly drain its liquidity, dump their tokens or abandon it after attracting buyers, leaving the token close to worthless."],["p2p-trading","P2P (C2C) trading","P2P (C2C) trading is buying or selling crypto directly with another user, paying in local money through bank transfer or payment apps while the platform holds the crypto in escrow."],["seed-phrase","Seed phrase (recovery phrase)","A seed phrase is a list of usually 12 or 24 ordinary words that encodes the master key of a crypto wallet, so anyone who has the words can restore the wallet and spend all of its funds."]],"lesson":{"id":"E11","slug":"scam-red-flags","ep":11,"title":"Someone says he can make you money. First, see what he wants."},"coin":null,"tool":null,"cta":false},"hash-rate":{"slug":"hash-rate","cat":"onchain","en":"Hash rate","contentLocale":"en","term":"Hash rate","aka":["hashrate","network hash rate","hash power"],"short":"Hash rate is the total computing power that miners use to secure a proof-of-work blockchain, measured in hashes per second.","body":[["In proof-of-work networks like ",["Bitcoin","bitcoin"],", miners repeatedly run a hashing function to find a valid block. Each attempt is one hash. The network hash rate estimates how many attempts all miners make per second, usually shown in exahashes per second (EH/s), where one exahash is a quintillion hashes."],"A higher hash rate makes the network harder to attack, because someone would need to control a large share of the total power to rewrite recent blocks. Bitcoin adjusts its mining difficulty roughly every 2,016 blocks so that blocks keep arriving about every 10 minutes no matter how much hash rate joins or leaves.","Hash rate is an estimate derived from how fast blocks are found, so daily readings are noisy; analysts often use 7-day or 30-day averages. Changes reflect miner economics: new machines, energy prices, regulation and events such as halvings, which cut miner rewards and can push less efficient miners offline. Hash rate is a security and health metric, not a direct price signal."],"example":"Suppose the Bitcoin network runs at 600 EH/s and a mining company operates machines totalling 12 EH/s. Its share is 12 / 600 = 2% of the network. With about 144 blocks per day and a reward of 3.125 BTC per block, total daily issuance is 450 BTC. A 2% share would earn about 9 BTC per day on average from block subsidies, before fees and costs, though actual results vary with luck.","mistakes":["Treating a single day's hash rate spike or drop as a real trend when estimates are noisy.","Assuming a rising hash rate means the price must rise.","Confusing hash rate with difficulty, which is adjusted periodically to respond to hash rate."],"faq":[{"q":"What does a high hash rate mean for Bitcoin?","a":"It means a lot of mining power is securing the network, making attacks more expensive. It also means more competition among miners for the same block rewards."},{"q":"Do proof-of-stake coins have a hash rate?","a":"No. Proof-of-stake chains such as Ethereum after September 2022 secure the network with staked coins and validators, not mining power."}],"title":"What is hash rate? Bitcoin mining power explained","description":"What hash rate means, how it is measured in EH/s, why it matters for Bitcoin security and how it links to mining difficulty.","related":[["mining","Crypto mining","Crypto mining is the process of using specialised computers to secure a proof-of-work blockchain by adding new blocks, in exchange for newly issued coins and transaction fees."],["proof-of-work","Proof of work (PoW)","Proof of work (PoW) is a consensus mechanism in which miners use computing power to solve a puzzle, and the first to find a valid answer earns the right to add the next block and collect the reward."],["bitcoin-halving","Bitcoin halving","A Bitcoin halving is a pre-programmed event, roughly every four years, that cuts the number of new bitcoins paid to miners for each block by 50 percent."],["on-chain-analysis","On-chain analysis","On-chain analysis is the study of public blockchain data, such as transactions, wallet balances and exchange flows, to understand how a network is used and how holders behave."]],"lesson":null,"coin":{"slug":"bitcoin","name":"Bitcoin","symbol":"BTC"},"tool":null,"cta":false},"exchange-netflow":{"slug":"exchange-netflow","cat":"onchain","en":"Exchange inflows and outflows","contentLocale":"en","term":"Exchange inflows and outflows","aka":["exchange netflow","exchange inflow","exchange outflow","exchange flows"],"short":"Exchange inflows and outflows measure how much of a coin moves onto and off centralized exchanges, and netflow is inflows minus outflows over a period.","body":["Analytics firms label wallets that belong to exchanges and track deposits and withdrawals. Inflow is the amount sent to exchange wallets, outflow is the amount sent out, and netflow is the difference. A positive netflow means more coins arrived than left; a negative one means more left than arrived.",["The usual reading is that coins sent to an exchange may be sold soon, while coins withdrawn to self-custody are more likely to be held. ",["Stablecoin","stablecoin"]," inflows are often read the other way: fresh ",["USDT","usdt"]," or ",["USDC","usdc"]," on exchanges can mean buying power waiting to be used."],["These are tendencies, not rules. Deposits can be used as margin for futures, moved for ",["arbitrage","arbitrage"],", or simply shuffled between an exchange's own wallets. Labels can be incomplete, especially for newer exchanges. A single large flow from one entity can dominate the data. It works best when you look at multi-day trends and compare them with price, funding rates and ",["open interest","open-interest"],"."]],"example":["Suppose over one week, 42,000 ",["ETH","ethereum"]," flowed into exchanges and 57,000 ETH flowed out. Netflow is 42,000 minus 57,000, which equals negative 15,000 ETH. If ETH trades around 3,000 USD, that is roughly 45 million USD of net withdrawals. On its own this suggests holders moved coins to storage, but you would still check whether a single exchange reshuffled wallets before drawing conclusions."],"mistakes":["Reading every large inflow as an immediate sell signal.","Ignoring internal exchange transfers that inflate inflow or outflow numbers.","Comparing netflow across data providers that use different wallet labels."],"faq":[{"q":"Is negative exchange netflow bullish?","a":"It means more coins left exchanges than arrived, which is often read as holders moving to self-custody. It is one data point, not a price guarantee."},{"q":"What does a stablecoin inflow to exchanges mean?","a":"It often suggests money is arriving that could be used to buy crypto or post margin. It can also simply be traders moving funds between venues."}],"title":"What is exchange netflow? Inflows and outflows explained","description":"Learn what exchange inflows, outflows and netflow mean, how analysts read them, and why wallet labels can distort the signal.","related":[["on-chain-analysis","On-chain analysis","On-chain analysis is the study of public blockchain data, such as transactions, wallet balances and exchange flows, to understand how a network is used and how holders behave."],["whale","Whale","A whale is an individual or entity that holds or trades such a large amount of a cryptocurrency that their moves can noticeably affect its price or liquidity."],["proof-of-reserves","Proof of reserves","Proof of reserves is a report in which a crypto exchange shows that the assets it holds in wallets it controls are at least equal to the balances it owes its customers."],["stablecoin","Stablecoin","A stablecoin is a cryptocurrency designed to keep a steady value, usually 1 US dollar, by being backed by reserves such as cash and treasury bills, by crypto collateral, or by other mechanisms."]],"lesson":null,"coin":null,"tool":"/onchain","cta":false},"active-addresses":{"slug":"active-addresses","cat":"onchain","en":"Active addresses","contentLocale":"en","term":"Active addresses","aka":["daily active addresses","DAA"],"short":"Active addresses are the number of unique blockchain addresses that sent or received a transaction during a given period, often counted per day.","body":["Active addresses are a simple way to gauge how much a network is being used. If more unique addresses transact each day, more activity is happening on-chain. Analysts track daily, weekly and monthly counts and compare them over time, not just across a single day.",["The metric has clear limits. One person or company can control thousands of addresses, while an exchange can serve millions of users through a few addresses. Bots, ",["airdrop","airdrop"]," farmers and spam transactions can inflate the count, especially on chains with very low fees. Some chains, like ",["Bitcoin","bitcoin"],", encourage using a new address per transaction, which also changes the numbers."],"Because of these quirks, active addresses work best as a trend within one chain. Comparing a low-fee chain with Bitcoin by raw address count is misleading. Pair the metric with fees paid, transaction value and the number of new addresses to get a clearer picture of real demand."],"example":"Suppose a network averaged 400,000 daily active addresses last month and 520,000 this month. That is an increase of 120,000, or 30%. But you also notice that 90,000 of the new addresses each received exactly 0.01 tokens from the same source, a pattern typical of an airdrop farming campaign. Removing them leaves 430,000, which is only 7.5% above last month.","mistakes":["Treating active addresses as the number of real users.","Comparing raw counts between chains with very different fee levels.","Ignoring spam or airdrop farming that can inflate address activity."],"faq":[{"q":"Do more active addresses mean higher prices?","a":"Not necessarily. Rising usage can reflect growing demand, but it can also come from spam, bots or incentive campaigns, so it is not a direct price signal."},{"q":"What counts as an active address?","a":"Usually any address that appears as a sender or receiver in a successful transaction during the period. Exact rules vary between data providers."}],"title":"What are active addresses? On-chain usage metric explained","description":"What active addresses measure, how analysts use them to gauge network activity, and why bots and airdrops can inflate the numbers.","related":[["on-chain-analysis","On-chain analysis","On-chain analysis is the study of public blockchain data, such as transactions, wallet balances and exchange flows, to understand how a network is used and how holders behave."],["wallet-address","Wallet address","A wallet address is a string of letters and numbers that identifies where crypto can be sent on a specific blockchain, similar to an account number you can share publicly."],["trading-volume","Trading volume","Trading volume is the total amount of an asset traded over a set period, usually shown as the 24-hour volume in coins or in USD value."],["total-value-locked","Total value locked (TVL)","Total value locked (TVL) is the total market value of crypto deposited in a DeFi protocol or blockchain's smart contracts, used as a rough measure of its size and usage."]],"lesson":null,"coin":null,"tool":"/onchain","cta":false},"fear-and-greed-index":{"slug":"fear-and-greed-index","cat":"onchain","en":"Fear and Greed Index","contentLocale":"en","term":"Fear and Greed Index","aka":["crypto fear and greed index","fear & greed index"],"short":"The Fear and Greed Index is a sentiment gauge that combines market data such as volatility, momentum and social activity into a single score from 0 (extreme fear) to 100 (extreme greed).","body":[["Crypto versions of the index, such as the widely followed one from Alternative.me, blend several inputs: recent ",["volatility","volatility"],", ",["trading volume","trading-volume"]," and momentum, social media activity, ",["bitcoin","bitcoin"]," dominance and search trends. Each provider weights them differently, so two indexes can show different scores on the same day."],"The score is usually grouped into bands: extreme fear, fear, neutral, greed and extreme greed. Many traders read it contrarian-style: extreme fear can appear near capitulation lows, and extreme greed can appear when the market is overheated and crowded with leveraged longs.",["Treat it as a mood thermometer, not a timing tool. Markets can stay in extreme greed for weeks during strong uptrends, and fear can deepen for a long time in bear markets. It is most useful when combined with things you can measure directly, such as funding rates, ",["open interest","open-interest"]," and your own ",["position size","position-sizing"],"."]],"example":["Suppose the index reads 85, extreme greed, while funding rates on major perpetual contracts have stayed positive for two weeks. You hold 1 BTC and planned to add another 0.5 BTC with 5x ",["leverage","leverage"],". Instead of adding at peak sentiment, you keep the 1 BTC and plan to buy the extra 0.5 BTC without leverage, in five spot orders of 0.1 BTC at lower levels. If the market keeps rising, you still hold your original 1 BTC; if it pulls back 15%, you avoid a leveraged position that would have lost about 75% of its margin."],"mistakes":["Buying or selling the moment the index hits an extreme, as if it were a precise signal.","Assuming all fear and greed indexes use the same formula.","Ignoring that the index is mostly built for bitcoin and may not reflect a specific altcoin."],"faq":[{"q":"Is extreme fear a good time to buy crypto?","a":"Some traders use extreme fear as a contrarian hint, but prices can keep falling while fear persists. It describes mood, not where the bottom is."},{"q":"How often is the Fear and Greed Index updated?","a":"Most crypto versions update once a day. Check the provider's page for its exact schedule and inputs."}],"title":"What is the Fear and Greed Index in crypto?","description":"What the crypto Fear and Greed Index measures, how its 0-100 score is built and how traders use sentiment without overtrusting it.","related":[["fomo","FOMO","FOMO, or fear of missing out, is the anxious urge to buy an asset after its price has already risen sharply, driven by the worry that others are profiting without you."],["fud","FUD","FUD, short for fear, uncertainty and doubt, describes negative news, rumours or opinions that make people worried about an asset, whether the concerns are valid or not."],["volatility","Volatility","Volatility is a measure of how much and how quickly an asset's price moves up and down over a given period, and higher volatility means larger, faster swings."],["funding-rate","Funding rate","A funding rate is a periodic payment between long and short traders that keeps a perpetual futures price close to the spot price."]],"lesson":null,"coin":null,"tool":"/onchain","cta":false},"referral-code":{"slug":"referral-code","cat":"exchange-features","en":"Referral code (fee rebate)","contentLocale":"en","term":"Referral code (fee rebate)","aka":["referral code","invite code","referral link","fee rebate code"],"short":"A referral code is a code you enter when signing up to an exchange that links your account to a referrer and can give you a discount or rebate on trading fees.","body":["Exchanges run referral and affiliate programs to attract users. When you register with a code, the referrer usually earns a share of the fees you pay, and in many programs part of that share is passed back to you as a fee discount, a rebate or a welcome reward. The exact split, duration and eligible products differ by exchange and by program.","A code must normally be entered at registration. On most exchanges it cannot be added later, so check before you create the account. Rebates may apply to spot, futures or both, may last for a limited period, and are often paid in a specific coin on a daily or weekly schedule.",["Use codes only from sources you trust and register on the exchange's official website or app. A legitimate code never needs your password, ",["seed phrase","seed-phrase"]," or API keys. A rebate lowers your trading cost, but it does not change the risk of the trades themselves."]],"example":["Suppose a futures ",["taker fee","maker-taker-fees"]," is 0.05% and your referral code gives a 20% rebate on fees. You trade 200,000 ",["USDT","usdt"]," of futures volume in a month. Your fees total 200,000 times 0.0005, which is 100 USDT. A 20% rebate returns 20 USDT, so your effective fee is 80 USDT, or 0.04%. These figures are illustrative; actual rates and rebates depend on the exchange and the program."],"mistakes":["Creating an account first and expecting to add a referral code later.","Signing up through a lookalike site that copied a referral link.","Trading more than planned just to earn a larger rebate."],"faq":[{"q":"Can I add a referral code after registering?","a":"On most exchanges, no. The code has to be entered during sign-up, though a few exchanges allow it within a short window afterwards."},{"q":"How does a referral fee rebate work?","a":"The exchange returns part of the trading fees you pay, usually as a percentage, credited to your account on a regular schedule."}],"title":"What is a referral code on crypto exchanges? Fee rebates","description":"How exchange referral codes work, how fee rebates are calculated, why codes must be entered at sign-up and how to use them safely.","related":[["maker-taker-fees","Maker and taker fees","Maker and taker fees are the two trading fee rates an exchange charges: a maker fee when your order adds liquidity to the order book, and a usually higher taker fee when your order removes it."],["vip-tier","VIP fee tier","A VIP fee tier is an exchange level that gives lower trading fees and extra benefits to users who trade high volume or hold large balances."],["centralized-exchange","Centralized exchange (CEX)","A centralized exchange (CEX) is a company-run trading platform where you deposit funds into an account and trade crypto through the exchange's order book, with the company holding custody of your assets."]],"lesson":null,"coin":null,"tool":"/rates","cta":true},"tokenized-treasuries":{"slug":"tokenized-treasuries","cat":"tokenized-assets","en":"Tokenized treasuries","contentLocale":"en","term":"Tokenized treasuries","aka":["tokenized T-bills","on-chain treasuries","tokenized US Treasuries"],"short":"Tokenized treasuries are blockchain tokens that represent shares in a fund or vehicle holding short-term government bonds such as US Treasury bills, passing their yield to holders.","body":["A fund manager buys government bonds, holds them with a custodian, and issues tokens that represent fund shares. The token earns the bond yield, minus fees, either by rising in price over time or by distributing new tokens. Examples include products from Ondo Finance and BlackRock's BUIDL fund.",["Their appeal is that ",["stablecoins","stablecoin"]," themselves usually do not pay interest to holders, while tokenized treasuries offer a yield linked to government rates and can still move on-chain. DAOs, crypto funds and some ",["DeFi protocols","defi"]," use them for treasury management or collateral."],["Many products are limited to qualified or non-US investors and require ",["KYC","kyc"]," and whitelisted wallets. Redemption may only be possible on business days and can take time. Yields follow central bank rates, so they change. ",["Smart contract","smart-contract"],", custodian and legal risks apply on top of the bonds themselves."]],"example":["Suppose a tokenized treasury fund holds T-bills yielding 4.5% a year and charges a 0.3% fee, giving a net yield of about 4.2%. You invest 20,000 ",["USDC","usdc"],". After 6 months, your holding grows by about 20,000 times 0.042 divided by 2, which is 420 USD, to about 20,420 USD. If rates fall to 3% later, the net yield drops to about 2.7% and future earnings shrink accordingly."],"mistakes":["Assuming a tokenized treasury is a stablecoin you can spend anywhere.","Ignoring investor eligibility rules and buying a token you cannot redeem.","Treating today's yield as fixed when it follows interest rates."],"faq":[{"q":"Are tokenized treasuries the same as stablecoins?","a":"No. Stablecoins aim to stay at a fixed value and usually pay no interest to holders. Tokenized treasuries pass on bond yield, and their value or token count grows over time."},{"q":"Who can buy tokenized treasuries?","a":"Many products are only open to verified or qualified investors in certain countries. Check the issuer's eligibility rules before buying."}],"title":"What are tokenized treasuries? On-chain T-bills","description":"How tokenized treasuries work, how they pass on government bond yields, who can buy them and how they differ from stablecoins.","related":[["real-world-assets","Real-world assets (RWA)","Real-world assets (RWA) are traditional assets such as bonds, gold, real estate, private credit or stocks represented as tokens on a blockchain."],["stablecoin","Stablecoin","A stablecoin is a cryptocurrency designed to keep a steady value, usually 1 US dollar, by being backed by reserves such as cash and treasury bills, by crypto collateral, or by other mechanisms."],["apr-vs-apy","APR vs APY","APR is the simple annual rate of return or interest without compounding, while APY includes the effect of reinvesting earnings, so APY is higher than APR whenever returns compound."],["kyc","KYC (know your customer)","KYC (know your customer) is the identity check that regulated exchanges and financial firms run before letting you deposit, trade or withdraw, usually with an ID document and a selfie."]],"lesson":null,"coin":{"slug":"ondo","name":"Ondo","symbol":"ONDO"},"tool":"/topics/tokenized-treasuries","cta":false}});
